<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cyprian</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 210–258</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a> · Also known as Cyprian of Carthage, Saint Cyprian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cyprian was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> from about 249 and one of the most influential <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> Christian writers. During the persecution of Decius he went into hiding and afterwards argued over how to readmit Christians who had lapsed. His writings on the unity of the church and the authority of bishops shaped later thought. He was beheaded near Carthage in 258 and is venerated as a saint.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cyprian.jpg" alt="Cyprian" width="258" height="327" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Bwag at German Wikipedia. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cyprian_von_Karthago2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
